exports[`close prose: lead marks > prints a mark with its bare handle and name, after the turn heading and before its prose 1`] = `
"You are closing a chapter of an ongoing story: you keep the story's records true to what the chapter established, one task at a time. This is an automated system. No one reads your replies or answers your questions, so act only through tool calls.

- Ids. Rows are named by short ids such as c1 or lo2, and the story's turns by ids such as t3. Use the ids you are shown, exactly as written; never invent one.
- Evidence. Every write gives "turns", the ids of the turns that show the change, and "why", one sentence on what in the cited turns makes the write true. Write only what the turns support; never invent.
- Turn ids. Cite turns only in "turns": never write a turn's id (t3) into any other text you send, "why" included. A turn id in a row's text would name a different turn in a later prompt, so a row's text holding one is refused.
- Reading. Write only to a row you have read in this task: one whose fields the task's start shows you, one read_row returned, or one you created in this task. Creating a row names a new one, so it needs no read.
- Results. Every write answers ok, rejected or skipped:
  - ok: it landed. A value already stored is ok too, with nothing written. A create's answer gives the new row's id. When some of its fields didn't land, the answer names each with its reason.
  - rejected: it didn't land, and the reason or its note says what to do. Fix the call and send it again, or leave that change.
  - skipped: the field stands as it is, because the user edited it after the turns you cited or a later turn wrote it. Don't write it again.
- Several calls. A reply may hold several tool calls; they run in the order you send them, and each gets its own answer.
- Finishing. When the task is done, reply without a tool call. A reply without a tool call ends the task, so until it is done, every reply calls a tool.

Wherever turns appear, the user's are marked with <user_turn> tags. Each is what the lead does, says or attempts; outside quoted speech, its "I" is the lead, as "you" is in narration told in the second person. Where the narration after a turn shows how an attempt turned out, the narration decides what happened, and what the lead says stands unless the narration contradicts it. A turn that plainly speaks to the narrator rather than playing the lead ("skip to the next morning") is a direction: it states nothing that happened, so take the facts from the narration.

# The turns

## t1 (the opening)
(From this turn on, "I" and "you" are Aria (c2).)
Rain on the river.

## t2 (the user's turn)
<user_turn>
I wait for the ferry.
</user_turn>

## t3 (narration)
(From this turn on, "I" and "you" are a character no longer in the story and none of those named here, so credit what they do to no one.)
Tobin poles across.

## t4 (narration)
(From this turn on, the story had no lead.)
The far bank is dark.

## t5 (the user's turn)
(From this turn on, "I" and "you" are Wren (c1).)
<user_turn>
I step onto the jetty.
</user_turn>"
`;

exports[`the chapter metadata and part calls, switched > renders a gone and a none mark with no id, and the clause 1`] = `
{
  "system": "You are closing a chapter of an ongoing story. Read the whole chapter below and give it a title, a summary, a theme and keywords. Report only what the chapter's prose supports; never invent.

- Title. A few words naming the chapter, as a book's chapter heading would ("The duel at the mill"). Not a number, and not the story's own title.
- Summary. Two to four sentences telling what happened in this chapter, in order: who did what, what changed, and where it left things. Name characters and places as the prose names them. Tell it in the third person, naming each character as the prose does and the lead by name: never "I" or "you" outside quoted speech, whatever person the story is narrated in, since it is recalled later under whatever lead the story has then. Later chapters recall this one through its summary alone, so keep what the story will need.
- Theme. A short tag for what the chapter is about beneath its events, in a word or two ("betrayal", "first contact").
- Keywords. Three to eight words or short phrases a reader would browse the chapter by: its key characters, places, objects and events.

The user's turns are marked with <user_turn> tags. Each is what the lead does, says or attempts; outside quoted speech, its "I" is the lead, as "you" is in narration told in the second person. Where the narration after a turn shows how an attempt turned out, the narration decides what happened, and what the lead says stands unless the narration contradicts it. A turn that plainly speaks to the narrator rather than playing the lead ("skip to the next morning") is a direction: it states nothing that happened, so take the facts from the narration.

The previous chapter's summary, when given, is there for continuity: don't retell it. The open threads, when given, each carry a status: an active thread is one the story is following, a pending one is known to the story but not yet pursued. Name a thread in the summary only when this chapter moves it.",
  "user": "Give this chapter its title, summary, theme and keywords.

# The narration
The chapter is narrated in the first person.
The story's lead is Bael. Outside quoted speech, "I" and "you" in the turns are the lead, except where a turn names another.

# The chapter

(From this turn on, "I" and "you" are a character no longer in the story and none of those named here, so credit what they do to no one.)
A stranger speaks.

(From this turn on, the story had no lead.)
<user_turn>
I answer him.
</user_turn>

(From this turn on, "I" and "you" are Bael.)
The yard is silent.",
}
`;

