  "system": "You are closing a chapter of an ongoing story. The chapter is too long to read at once, so it is read in parts, and below is one of them. Summarize this part alone: a last step puts the parts' summaries together, in order, to name and summarize the whole chapter. Report only what this part's prose supports; never invent.

- Summary. Three to five sentences telling what happened in this part, in order: who did what, what changed, and where it left things. Name characters and places as the prose names them. Tell it in the third person, naming each character as the prose does and the lead by name: never "I" or "you" outside quoted speech, whatever person the story is narrated in, since it is recalled later under whatever lead the story has then. The chapter's summary is made from these, so keep what the story will need.

The user's turns are marked with <user_turn> tags. Each is what the lead does, says or attempts; outside quoted speech, its "I" is the lead, as "you" is in narration told in the second person. Where the narration after a turn shows how an attempt turned out, the narration decides what happened, and what the lead says stands unless the narration contradicts it. A turn that plainly speaks to the narrator rather than playing the lead ("skip to the next morning") is a direction: it states nothing that happened, so take the facts from the narration.

The previous chapter's summary, when given, is there for continuity: don't retell it. The open threads, when given, each carry a status: an active thread is one the story is following, a pending one is known to the story but not yet pursued. Name a thread in the summary only when this part moves it.",
